# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  The Boogeyman: keys and settings
#
#  1. Copy this file:        make env      (or: cp .env.example .env)
#  2. Paste your keys into .env (NOT this file).
#  3. Restart the app (make dev).
#
#  .env is gitignored (so is brain/.env, which also still works). Keys stay on your machine and never reach the UI.
#  Never commit a real key, and never paste one into an issue or chat.
#
#  Minimum to get him talking: ELEVENLABS_API_KEY + GROQ_API_KEY (both free).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── 1. VOICE: ElevenLabs (hearing you + speaking). Required for voice. ───────
#
#  Get it (free plan is enough):
#    1. Sign up at https://elevenlabs.io
#    2. Profile (bottom left) → API Keys → Create API key
#    3. Under permissions, give it at least:
#         Text to Speech: Access
#         Speech to Text: Access
#         Voices:         Read
#    4. Copy the key (starts with sk_) and paste it below.
#
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# The voice he speaks with. Default is "Brian" (deep, premade, works on the free plan).
# To use another one: open a voice in your ElevenLabs Voices page → copy its Voice ID.
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=nPczCjzI2devNBz1zQrb

# Models (defaults are fast and cheap; leave as is unless you know why).
# ELEVENLABS_TTS_MODEL=eleven_flash_v2_5
# ELEVENLABS_STT_MODEL=scribe_v2


# ── 2. BRAIN: the AI that thinks and replies. Set at least ONE. ──────────────
#
#  Without any of these he still hears and speaks, but only knows a few
#  built-in lines and the instant commands (shut down, work mode, etc.).
#
#  Groq (free, fast, recommended to start):
#    1. Sign up at https://console.groq.com
#    2. API Keys → Create API Key
#    3. Copy the key (starts with gsk_) and paste it below.
#  The free tier has per-minute and per-day limits; when one model runs out he
#  automatically moves to the next free model.
#
GROQ_API_KEY=

#  Anthropic Claude (paid, smartest). Get a key at https://console.anthropic.com
#  If this is set, he uses Claude first (with LLM_PROVIDER=auto).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

#  Which brain to use: auto (Anthropic if set, else Groq) | groq | anthropic | openai
# LLM_PROVIDER=auto

#  Override the model. Defaults: openai/gpt-oss-120b on Groq, claude-haiku-5-5 on Anthropic.
# LLM_MODEL=

#  Any other OpenAI-compatible API (OpenRouter, Gemini, a local server...):
# LLM_PROVIDER=openai
# LLM_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# LLM_API_KEY=
# LLM_MODEL=


# ── 3. OPTIONAL TUNING ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Microphone by name or index. List them with:  brain/.venv/bin/python -m sounddevice
# BOOGEYMAN_MIC=

# 0 turns voice off entirely (UI only).
# BOOGEYMAN_VOICE=1

# Talking over him makes him stop and listen. If he cuts himself off on loud
# speakers, raise the level (e.g. 1.5) or turn it off with 0. Headphones fix it.
# BOOGEYMAN_BARGE_IN=1
# BOOGEYMAN_BARGE_LEVEL=1.0

# How many recent messages go with each reply (fewer = lasts longer on free limits).
# BOOGEYMAN_HISTORY=12

# Where memory.json lives (default: ~/.local/share/boogeyman).
# BOOGEYMAN_DATA=

# Research (web search) uses DuckDuckGo through the ddgs package: free, no key needed.


# ── 4. FREE VOICE (no key, no credit card) ───────────────────────────────────
#
#  ElevenLabs sounds best, but the free plan is 10,000 characters a month and
#  then he goes silent. These two cost nothing and need no key of their own:
#
#    Voice  Microsoft Edge neural voices (edge-tts). 300+ voices, no account.
#    Ears   Whisper large v3 turbo on Groq, using the GROQ_API_KEY above.
#    Voice  Orpheus (open source, Apache-2.0) on Groq, on that same key. Opt-in
#           only: BOOGEYMAN_TTS=groq. It was tried as the faster option and it
#           isn't. Measured against Edge on the same short reply:
#             synth    954ms  vs 1560ms   Orpheus starts ~600ms sooner
#             speech   4.96s  vs  3.53s   then talks 1.43s longer
#             total    5.91s  vs  5.09s   so Edge wins by ~800ms a reply
#           Orpheus reads at 9.5 chars/sec against Edge's 13.3 on that line and
#           cannot be pushed: `speed` returns 200 and is silently ignored (0.5,
#           1.5 and the default give byte-identical audio). The rate knob is what
#           made Edge quick, and Orpheus has no equivalent. The free tier also
#           allows 100 requests per 4h window, and each reply spends 2-4 of them.
#           Its terms must be accepted once, free, at
#           console.groq.com/playground?model=canopylabs/orpheus-v1-english
#           Before that every request is a 400 (code model_terms_required).
#
#  Which to use. "auto" (the default) works it out at boot: it checks what each
#  key is actually allowed to do and picks the best voice that works, so a spent
#  ElevenLabs quota or a locked Orpheus costs you nothing at speaking time.
#  Force one with "elevenlabs" / "groq" / "edge" for the voice, and
#  "elevenlabs" / "groq" for the ears.
# BOOGEYMAN_TTS=auto
# BOOGEYMAN_STT=auto

#  His free voice. See them all with:  edge-tts --list-voices
#  There is no Ghanaian voice in Edge - the West African locales stop at Nigerian,
#  Kenyan, Tanzanian and South African - so there is no accent to match, and the pick
#  comes down to reading pace and taste. Default is Brian Multilingual: joint-fastest
#  reader of the 23 English male voices and newest-generation, so it sounds natural
#  without being pushed hard.
#  Also quick: en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural (warmer), en-AU-WilliamNeural,
#  en-ZA-LukeNeural. Slow but characterful: en-US-ChristopherNeural (deepest of the
#  lot, and about 2s longer per reply), en-US-SteffanNeural (level narrator).
# EDGE_VOICE=en-US-BrianMultilingualNeural

#  How fast he talks. Unpushed, these voices read at 12 to 17.4 characters a second,
#  which at the low end sounds like a man reading off a card. Raise it if he still
#  drags; lower it if he trips over the Pidgin.
#  This number belongs to the voice, not to him, so re-measure if you change
#  EDGE_VOICE. It is also the wrong knob to lean on: a slow voice cranked far enough
#  to keep up sounds artificial. Steffan needed +53% to reach the pace Brian
#  Multilingual reaches at +30%. Pick a quick reader first, then push it gently.
#  Measured on this voice: +30% is 5.57s for a two-sentence reply, +40% is 5.16s,
#  +50% is 4.82s, +60% is 4.51s. Past +40% the returns shrink and it starts to
#  sound wound up, so that is the default; samples of each are easy to re-render.
# EDGE_RATE=+40%

#  A single Edge request occasionally hangs long past its usual 1.6s. Rather
#  than wait it out, give up and ask again - a fresh one comes straight back.
# EDGE_TIMEOUT=6
# EDGE_TRIES=3

#  Devil mode: a different voice, or just slower and pitched down. The rate is
#  absolute, not relative to EDGE_RATE, so it should sit below it - and if you raise
#  EDGE_RATE, the gap to this one widens, which makes devil mode drag further.
#  A separate voice is the better trick: keep a quick one above for normal talk and
#  put the slow, deep one (en-US-ChristopherNeural) here, where the menace is wanted
#  and the extra seconds are rare.
# EDGE_DEVIL_VOICE=
# EDGE_DEVIL_RATE=+4%
# EDGE_DEVIL_PITCH=-25Hz

#  Whisper model on Groq (free tier).
# GROQ_STT_MODEL=whisper-large-v3-turbo

#  Orpheus voices, as Groq actually serves them: autumn diana hannah austin
#  daniel troy. NOT the open-source model card's list (tara/leo/zac/...), which
#  returns a 400 - austin, daniel and troy are the male ones.
# GROQ_TTS_VOICE=austin
# GROQ_TTS_DEVIL_VOICE=daniel

#  Claude thinks before answering unless told not to. On a voice turn that is
#  pure delay - measured at 146 output tokens of deliberation to produce "doing
#  fine, ready when you are", and 748ms of silence before the first word. Set
#  this to 1 if you'd rather have the reasoning than the second.
# BOOGEYMAN_THINK=0


# ── 5. EYES: reading your screen ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#  Needs mss and pillow (both in requirements.txt) and a brain that can see.
#  Anthropic can; on Groq the text models can't, so a look uses the vision model
#  named below instead of the usual free chain.
#
#  Two features use this:
#    look_at_screen  a tool he can reach for on his own, when you ask what
#                    something on your screen says, means, or why it's failing.
#    study mode      say "study mode" (or "watch my screen") and he goes quiet
#                    until there's a question in front of him, then gives the
#                    answer and one short line of why. "Further more" opens the
#                    last one up properly. "Stop studying" ends it.
#
#  Which screen, when you have more than one. 0 is the primary; 1, 2... are the
#  individual screens as `python -c "import mss;print(mss.MSS().monitors)"` lists
#  them. Never the union of all of them, which would letterbox the picture.
# BOOGEYMAN_SCREEN=0

#  How a screenshot is sent. 1568px is where Anthropic downscales anyway, and
#  JPEG q92 was measured at 262KB against PNG's 862KB for the same screen - with
#  no loss that mattered: asked to transcribe 11-pixel footer text and a random
#  serial number off a rendered quiz, the model read both exactly from q85 up.
# BOOGEYMAN_SCREEN_EDGE=1568
# BOOGEYMAN_SCREEN_QUALITY=92

#  Study mode's pacing. Looking costs a model call; noticing costs 63ms, so the
#  loop only senses (a 48x48 grey grid) and spends a look when the screen has
#  moved and then held still.
#    POLL      seconds between senses
#    MOVED     how much the grid must move to count as a change. Small on
#              purpose: a quiz app swapping the question inside an identical
#              layout only moves it about 0.0016, while a blinking caret moves
#              it 0.00003. Raising this to something that "looks safe" is how
#              you get a Boogeyman that silently never sees question two.
#    SETTLE    senses it must hold still for, so a half-drawn page isn't read
#    COOLDOWN  floor between looks, whatever the screen does
#    SWEEP     he looks anyway this often. Two questions can carry the same
#              amount of text, and that swap hides under MOVED; this catches it.
#              The dedup means a sweep costs a look, never a repeated answer.
# BOOGEYMAN_STUDY_POLL=0.3
# BOOGEYMAN_STUDY_MOVED=0.0005
# BOOGEYMAN_STUDY_SETTLE=2
# BOOGEYMAN_STUDY_COOLDOWN=1.2
# BOOGEYMAN_STUDY_SWEEP=30

#  Vision model for the Groq brain (Anthropic uses its own model either way).
# GROQ_VISION_MODEL=meta-llama/llama-4-scout-17b-16e-instruct

#  Room for one screen answer: a verdict plus a handful of words of why. Kept
#  tight on purpose - the explanation is what he spends his time saying, and
#  cutting it from 20 words to 10 took an answer from 5.1s of speech to 2.5s,
#  which is seven times more than any speaking-rate change was worth.
# BOOGEYMAN_VISION_TOKENS=200
